Yes. During the wartime period—especially the late 1930s and 1940s—Korean laborers worked on Hashima under coercive conditions that historians generally describe as forced or compulsory labor.

Hashima was a coal-mining island owned by Mitsubishi, and during World War II Japan faced severe labor shortages. Because Korea was under Japanese colonial rule, the Japanese state and companies mobilized Korean workers through a mix of deceptive recruiting, official labor requisition, conscription-style mobilization, and company control.

What made it coercive or forced included:

- workers were often lured with false promises or pressured by officials and recruiters;
- once assigned, they could not freely quit or leave;
- they worked long, dangerous shifts in the mine;
- they were subjected to surveillance, punishment, poor food, and harsh living conditions;
- wages were often controlled, deducted, or not fully accessible;
- the island’s isolation made departure difficult.

This fits the core international definition of forced labor: work exacted under menace or compulsion and not entered into voluntarily.

There is survivor testimony, historical research, and Japanese local/company records documenting Korean labor on Hashima. In 2015, when Hashima was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage site, Japan’s delegation acknowledged that large numbers of Koreans and others were “brought against their will and forced to work under harsh conditions” at some Meiji industrial sites in the 1940s.

The terminology remains politically disputed. Some Japanese accounts prefer terms like “mobilized labor” and emphasize that wages were paid, while Korean victims and many historians argue that the system was effectively forced labor. But the historical evidence strongly supports that Korean forced or coerced labor took place on Hashima during the wartime period.
